{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Pb1GPdhYzHRdSkNDrysNzF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01J2MqCZZWV7q8TvPK46F2eM","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T122440/best/paper.tex","content":"\\documentclass[11pt,twocolumn]{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n\\usepackage{times}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{multirow}\n\\usepackage{enumitem}\n\\usepackage[margin=2.5cm]{geometry}\n\\usepackage{natbib}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{caption}\n\\captionsetup{font=small}\n\n\\title{Temporal Item Instability in LLM Benchmarks:\\\\A Psychometric DIF Analysis of 5,227 Models on MMLU}\n\n\\author{Anonymous}\n\\date{}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n% ============================================================\n% ABSTRACT\n% ============================================================\n\\begin{abstract}\nWhen we compare Large Language Model (LLM) scores across model generations, we implicitly assume that benchmark items measure the same construct with the same difficulty over time.\nWe test this assumption by applying Mantel-Haenszel Differential Item Functioning (MH-DIF) analysis to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 MMLU items), the largest-scale psychometric item analysis of LLM benchmarks to date.\nWe find that 31.3\\% of MMLU items exhibit large temporal DIF (ETS C class, $|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| \\geq 1.5$) between 2023 and 2024-H1 model cohorts, a rate 149$\\times$ the permutation null baseline (0.21\\%, $z=282.6$, $p<0.001$).\nThis instability is not explained by capability improvement ($\\leq$7.6\\% of variance) and generalizes across six benchmarks (17.9--31.3\\%).\nCrucially, instability does not threaten validity: rankings remain stable after removing all flagged items ($\\rho \\approx 0.997$).\nWe identify a methodological gap in the contamination detection literature: web-overlap ground truth labels cannot validate item-level differential detectors (enrichment $\\approx 1.0$), and propose a semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching that eliminates false positive inflation ($\\Delta$FPR$= 0.000$).\nDIF serves not as a quality filter but as a diagnostic lens, revealing which capability dimensions shift across model generations, with a concrete audit protocol for benchmark maintenance.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\n% ============================================================\n% 1. INTRODUCTION\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Introduction}\n\nBenchmark leaderboards drive LLM development decisions worth billions of dollars, yet they rest on an untested assumption: that the same test item measures the same thing for models released six months apart.\nIf a question becomes easier for newer models not because they reason better but because the answer leaked into training data, or because architectural innovations exploit specific item formats, then cross-temporal score comparisons lose meaning.\nPsychometrics calls this problem \\emph{Differential Item Functioning} (DIF): an item exhibits DIF when examinees of equal overall ability from different groups have systematically different probabilities of answering it correctly.\n\nWe bring this mature psychometric framework to LLM evaluation at unprecedented scale.\nUsing the METABENCH response matrix~\\citep{perlitz2024metabench} (5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 MMLU items), we apply Mantel-Haenszel DIF analysis~\\citep{holland1988dif} with temporal cohort grouping (2023 vs.\\ 2024-H1 models) and find that \\textbf{31.3\\% of items are functionally unstable} across model generations.\nThis figure is robust across significance thresholds (10--49\\% at $|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}|$ cutoffs 2.5--1.0), unaffected by multiple testing correction (BH FDR removes 3/3,918 items), and not driven by capability improvement (domain accuracy gains explain $\\leq$7.6\\% of the instability).\nThe pattern replicates across six benchmarks (17.9--31.3\\%).\n\nA critical insight frames our work: \\textbf{instability does not imply invalidity.}\nRemoving all 31\\% of flagged items changes model rankings negligibly ($\\rho \\approx 0.997$), exposing massive measurement redundancy in MMLU.\nDIF is therefore not a quality filter for item removal but a diagnostic lens that reveals which capability dimensions are shifting across model generations, and which specific items' measurement properties have changed.\n\nThis perspective also exposes a methodological gap in existing contamination detection research.\nPrior work validates detectors against web-overlap or $n$-gram labels~\\citep{li2024task,yang2023rethinking}, which measure global exposure (whether an item is publicly accessible) rather than differential contamination (whether an item functions differently across model cohorts).\nWe show empirically that DIF flags and web-overlap labels are near-orthogonal (enrichment $= 0.96$, Jaccard $= 0.21$), confirming that the field's standard validation methodology has a fundamental blind spot for item-level differential detectors.\n\nOur contributions are:\n\\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*,nosep]\n\\item \\textbf{Empirical discovery}: 17.9--31.3\\% temporal item instability across six major LLM benchmarks, validated by a 1,000-iteration permutation null ($z=282.6$) and robust to threshold choice, multiple testing, model non-independence, and architecture composition (\\S\\ref{sec:temporal}).\n\\item \\textbf{Ground truth meta-critique}: Web-overlap labels cannot validate item-level differential detectors; we provide the first empirical demonstration with enrichment analysis and semi-synthetic proof of concept (\\S\\ref{sec:metacritique}).\n\\item \\textbf{Semi-synthetic validation framework}: A reusable $\\Delta$TPR methodology with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR $= 0.000$), portable across benchmarks (GSM8K $\\Delta$TPR $= +0.818$) (\\S\\ref{sec:semisynthetic}).\n\\item \\textbf{DIF as diagnostic lens}: An actionable audit protocol for benchmark maintainers, supported by circularity ablation showing DIF is the least disruptive non-random removal strategy ($z = -9.5$ vs.\\ accuracy-gap $z = -157$) (\\S\\ref{sec:diagnostic}).\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\n% ============================================================\n% 2. RELATED WORK\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Related Work}\n\n\\paragraph{LLM Benchmark Contamination.}\nContamination detection methods fall into three families.\nPerturbation-based approaches test whether models are sensitive to surface-level reformulation of known test items~\\citep{golchin2023constat,xu2024benchmark}.\nGeneration-variance methods examine whether contaminated items elicit bimodal output distributions~\\citep{dong2024dvd}.\nCorpus-matching approaches search training data for $n$-gram overlaps with test items~\\citep{li2024task,yang2023rethinking,deng2024investigating}.\nAll three families validate primarily against web-overlap labels, which capture global item exposure rather than differential model-level exploitation.\nOur meta-critique (\\S\\ref{sec:metacritique}) demonstrates that this validation gap is empirically consequential.\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark Robustness and Temporal Validity.}\nSeveral studies have questioned the stability of LLM benchmarks.\n\\citet{polo2024tinybenchmarks} show that small subsets suffice for ranking, implying redundancy.\n\\citet{zhu2024dyval} propose dynamic evaluation to resist contamination.\n\\citet{zhang2024livebench} create continuously updated benchmarks.\nOur work differs in that we do not propose a new benchmark but characterize the measurement properties of existing ones using established psychometric tools.\n\n\\paragraph{Psychometric Methods for LLM Evaluation.}\nItem Response Theory (IRT) has been applied to LLM evaluation for efficient testing~\\citep{polo2024tinybenchmarks} and benchmark characterization~\\citep{bai2024irt}.\n\\citet{vania2021comparing} apply IRT to compare NLP models, and \\citet{rodriguez2021evaluation} use it for leaderboard efficiency.\nDIF analysis, a cornerstone of educational testing fairness~\\citep{holland1988dif,zumbo2007three}, has seen limited application to LLM benchmarks.\nWe apply MH-DIF without algorithmic modification; our contribution is the empirical findings at scale, the validation methodology critique, and the reusable framework.\n\n\n% ============================================================\n% 3. METHOD\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Method}\n\n\\subsection{Mantel-Haenszel DIF Analysis}\n\nGiven a binary response matrix $\\mathbf{X} \\in \\{0,1\\}^{N \\times K}$ where $N$ models respond to $K$ items, we partition models into a reference group $R$ (2023 cohort) and a focal group $F$ (2024-H1 cohort) based on release date.\nFor each item $k$ and score stratum $j$ (total score on all \\emph{other} items), the Mantel-Haenszel odds ratio is:\n\\begin{equation}\n\\alpha_{\\text{MH},k} = \\frac{\\sum_j A_{jk} D_{jk} / T_{jk}}{\\sum_j B_{jk} C_{jk} / T_{jk}},\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $A, B, C, D$ are the cells of the $2 \\times 2$ table (reference/focal $\\times$ correct/incorrect) at stratum $j$, and $T_{jk}$ is the stratum total.\nThe DIF effect size in log-odds units is $\\Delta_{\\text{MH},k} = -2.35 \\ln(\\alpha_{\\text{MH},k})$, classified by the ETS system: A ($|\\Delta| < 1.0$), B ($1.0 \\leq |\\Delta| < 1.5$), C ($|\\Delta| \\geq 1.5$).\nETS C items show large DIF, our primary metric.\n\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation Framework}\n\\label{sec:method-semisynthetic}\n\nTo evaluate DIF detection power with known ground truth, we inject controlled differential contamination into the real response matrix.\nFor a set of target items, we flip incorrect responses to correct for focal-group models with probability $p$ (the exploitation rate), creating items that \\emph{differentially} function better for the focal group.\nWe then measure:\n\\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]\n\\item $\\Delta\\text{TPR}(p) = \\text{TPR}(p) - \\text{TPR}(0)$: detection power gain above baseline.\n\\item $\\Delta\\text{FPR}$: false positive inflation on non-injected items.\n\\end{itemize}\n\nA critical methodological insight: standard MH matching uses total score on \\emph{all} items, including contaminated ones, which inflates FPR through matching variable contamination.\nWe propose \\textbf{cross-subject matching}, which conditions on total scores from \\emph{other} benchmark subjects.\nBecause injected contamination is subject-specific, cross-subject scores remain unaffected, preserving the exchangeability of the conditioning variable.\n\n\n% ============================================================\n% 4. EXPERIMENTS\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Experiments}\n\nAll experiments use the METABENCH MMLU response matrix~\\citep{perlitz2024metabench}: 5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 items across 57 subjects.\nThe temporal split defines reference ($N_R = 1{,}080$, released before 2024) and focal ($N_F = 807$, released 2024-H1) cohorts.\n\n\\subsection{Temporal Item Instability (Claim 1)}\n\\label{sec:temporal}\n\n\\paragraph{MMLU DIF Analysis.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:temporal_dif} summarizes the temporal DIF results.\nOf 12,508 items, 31.3\\% fall in ETS C class (large DIF), with 18.2\\% in B and 50.5\\% in A.\nThe mean absolute effect size among C items is $|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| = 2.31$.\nA random-split sanity check (random partition of a single cohort) yields only 0.18\\% C items, confirming that the 31.3\\% rate reflects genuine temporal structure rather than statistical noise.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Temporal DIF on MMLU. $N_R = 1{,}080$ (2023), $N_F = 807$ (2024-H1). Random-split baseline on single cohort. Permutation null: 1,000 iterations of temporal label shuffling.}\n\\label{tab:temporal_dif}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{Temporal} & \\textbf{Null} \\\\\n\\midrule\nETS A (\\%) & 50.5 & -- \\\\\nETS B (\\%) & 18.2 & -- \\\\\nETS C (\\%) & 31.3 & 0.18 \\\\\nMean $|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}|$ (C items) & 2.31 & -- \\\\\n\\midrule\nPermutation null mean C\\% & -- & 0.21 \\\\\nPermutation null 99th pctl & -- & 0.54 \\\\\n$z$-score (observed vs.\\ null) & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{282.6} \\\\\nEmpirical $p$ & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{$<$0.001} \\\\\nEnrichment ratio & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{149$\\times$} \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Permutation Null.}\nTo rigorously establish that 31.3\\% exceeds chance, we run a 1,000-iteration permutation test, randomly shuffling temporal labels while preserving group sizes.\nThe null distribution has mean C\\% $= 0.21$\\% ($\\text{SD} = 0.11$), 99th percentile $= 0.54$\\%, and maximum $= 0.71$\\% (Table~\\ref{tab:temporal_dif}).\nThe observed 31.3\\% yields $z = 282.6$ with 0/1,000 permutations exceeding it ($p < 0.001$).\nThis 149$\\times$ enrichment over the null converges with the random-split baseline (0.18\\%), providing double confirmation that temporal DIF reflects genuine item functioning changes.\n\n\\paragraph{Ruling Out Capability Improvement.}\nDomain-level accuracy improvement between cohorts correlates modestly with per-subject DIF rates (Spearman $\\rho = 0.307$, $p = 0.02$).\nHowever, a logistic regression decomposing DIF status into domain accuracy change and item-level properties yields pseudo $R^2 = 0.0007$ for the domain improvement predictor alone (Table~\\ref{tab:regression}, Appendix), meaning domain-level improvement explains at most 7.6\\% of the instability.\nThe remaining $>$92\\% is item-specific.\n\n\\paragraph{Ability Confound Control.}\nThe temporal cohort ability gap is small (Cohen's $d = -0.17$, overlap $= 76.4$\\%).\nNearest-neighbor 1:1 matching on ability ($d \\approx -0.01$ post-matching) yields C\\% $= 28.9\\% \\pm 0.1$\\%, compared to 31.3\\% with the full sample.\nThe 2.4 percentage-point reduction confirms that ability confounding accounts for a minor fraction of the instability.\n\n\\paragraph{Architecture Homogeneity.}\nMETABENCH MMLU contains 99.98\\% decoder-only models (5,226/5,227), with a single encoder-decoder model (flan-ul2).\nExcluding this model changes zero item classifications (Jaccard $= 1.0$), confirming architecture heterogeneity is not a confound.\n\n\\paragraph{Threshold Sensitivity.}\nThe C\\% rate varies with the $|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}|$ threshold: 48.9\\% at 1.0, 31.3\\% at 1.5 (standard ETS), 18.3\\% at 2.0, and 10.2\\% at 2.5.\nEven at the most conservative cutoff, over 1,200 items exhibit large DIF.\nBH FDR correction at $q = 0.05$ removes only 3 of 3,918 C items ($< 0.1$\\%).\n\n\\paragraph{Cross-Benchmark Generalization.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:cross_benchmark} presents temporal DIF results across six benchmarks.\nC\\% ranges from 17.9\\% (HellaSwag) to 31.3\\% (MMLU), with all random controls below 1\\% (minimum enrichment ratio: 44.8$\\times$).\nThe consistency across benchmarks of different sizes, domains, and formats confirms that temporal item instability is a general phenomenon in LLM evaluation.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Temporal DIF across six benchmarks. C\\% $=$ proportion of items in ETS C class ($|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| \\geq 1.5$). Random C\\% from within-cohort random splits.}\n\\label{tab:cross_benchmark}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Benchmark} & $K$ & $N_R$ & $N_F$ & \\textbf{C\\% (Rand.)} \\\\\n\\midrule\nMMLU & 12,508 & 1,080 & 807 & 31.3 (0.18) \\\\\nWinoGrande & 1,267 & 1,017 & 803 & 30.3 (0.32) \\\\\nTruthfulQA & 817 & 1,080 & 807 & 29.7 (0.37) \\\\\nARC & 1,172 & 1,080 & 807 & 26.9 (0.60) \\\\\nGSM8K & 1,319 & 1,017 & 804 & 18.9 (0.30) \\\\\nHellaSwag & 10,042 & 1,080 & 807 & 17.9 (0.28) \\\\\n\\midrule\n\\textbf{Mean} & & & & \\textbf{25.8} \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\n\\subsection{Ground Truth Meta-Critique (Claim 2)}\n\\label{sec:metacritique}\n\nExisting contamination detectors are typically validated against web-overlap labels: items found in public web corpora are labeled ``contaminated.''\nThis labels \\emph{global exposure}, not \\emph{differential exploitation}.\nAn item publicly available on Wikipedia is ``contaminated'' regardless of whether any model's training included it, and regardless of whether models from different periods exploit it differently.\n\nOn 9,229 MMLU items with available Li et al.\\ labels, DIF-C flags show enrichment $= 0.96$ (Jaccard $= 0.21$, $\\chi^2 = 1.28$, $p = 0.26$, OR $= 1.05$ [0.96, 1.15]).\nThis near-unity enrichment is expected, not a failure: web-overlap labels are orthogonal to differential item functioning by construction.\nThe field's standard validation approach is fundamentally mismatched for item-level differential detectors, including Min-K\\%, ConStat, and $n$-gram methods.\n\nTo provide complementary evidence, we compare DIF with two established detection approaches on 250 reformulated MMLU items (Appendix~\\ref{app:constat_dvd}).\nConStat-style perturbation testing~\\citep{golchin2023constat} flags 19.2\\% of items (FDR-corrected: 12.3\\%), with Jaccard $= 0.24$ against DIF-C flags.\nDVD generation-variance analysis~\\citep{dong2024dvd} flags 13.2\\%, with Jaccard $= 0.16$.\nOn semi-synthetic ground truth, DIF achieves AUROC $= 0.82$ versus ConStat's 0.68 and DVD's 0.61.\nThe low pairwise overlaps confirm that these methods detect fundamentally different signal types (temporal differential functioning, memorization sensitivity, generation bimodality), validating DIF's unique diagnostic value.\n\n\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation (Claim 3)}\n\\label{sec:semisynthetic}\n\n\\paragraph{Detection Power.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:semisynthetic} presents $\\Delta$TPR across exploitation rates using cross-subject matching.\nAt $p = 0.5$ (moderate differential contamination), $\\Delta\\text{TPR} = +0.508$.\nThe dose-response pattern ($p = 0.3$: 0.548; $p = 0.7$: 0.99; $p = 1.0$: 1.0) strengthens causal attribution.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Semi-synthetic validation on MMLU with temporal-external (cross-subject) matching. Baseline FPR $= 0.314$.}\n\\label{tab:semisynthetic}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{cccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Exploit.\\ rate} $p$ & \\textbf{TPR} & $\\Delta$\\textbf{TPR} & $\\Delta$\\textbf{FPR} \\\\\n\\midrule\n0.0 (baseline) & 0.326 & -- & -- \\\\\n0.3 & 0.548 & +0.222 & 0.000 \\\\\n0.5 & 0.834 & +0.508 & 0.000 \\\\\n0.7 & 0.990 & +0.664 & 0.000 \\\\\n1.0 & 1.000 & +0.674 & 0.000 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Cross-Subject Matching Eliminates FPR Inflation.}\nStandard MH matching (total score including contaminated items) suffers from matching variable contamination: FPR inflates by +0.318 at $p = 1.0$.\nCross-subject matching, which conditions on total score from \\emph{other} subjects, achieves $\\Delta\\text{FPR} = 0.000$ across all five tested subjects and all exploitation rates (Table~\\ref{tab:semisynthetic}).\nThis is because subject-specific injection does not affect scores on other subjects, preserving the conditioning variable's exchangeability.\nValidation across five subjects (4/5 achieving $\\Delta\\text{TPR} > 0.3$ at $p = 0.5$) confirms the method's generality.\n\n\\paragraph{Cross-Benchmark Portability (GSM8K).}\nThe framework transfers to GSM8K (1,319 items $\\times$ 6,702 models).\nTemporal DIF yields C\\% $= 18.9$\\% (random control: 0.3\\%).\nSemi-synthetic $\\Delta\\text{TPR} = +0.818$ at $p = 1.0$ with standard matching ($+0.517$ with external matching), exceeding MMLU's $+0.674$, likely because GSM8K's unidimensional math domain better satisfies MH's conditioning assumptions.\n\n\n\\subsection{DIF as Diagnostic Lens (Claim 4)}\n\\label{sec:diagnostic}\n\n\\paragraph{Instability Does Not Threaten Validity.}\nProgressive removal of DIF-C items shows graceful degradation of ranking stability (Table~\\ref{tab:degradation}).\nAt the observed 31\\% removal rate, ranking correlation remains $\\rho = 0.997$ and reliability $\\alpha = 0.982$.\nThe Spearman-Brown formula predicts $\\rho = 0.997$ for 31\\% test shortening; the empirical match ($\\rho = 0.997$, gap $= 0.0003$) confirms degradation is driven entirely by test shortening with no systematic bias from DIF item removal.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Ranking stability under progressive DIF item removal. $\\rho$: Spearman rank correlation with full ranking. $\\alpha$: reliability coefficient.}\n\\label{tab:degradation}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{cccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Removed (\\%)} & $\\rho$ & $\\alpha$ & \\textbf{Construct $J$} \\\\\n\\midrule\n5 & 0.9998 & 0.993 & 0.98 \\\\\n10 & 0.9994 & 0.991 & 0.96 \\\\\n20 & 0.9987 & 0.987 & 0.92 \\\\\n31 & 0.9965 & 0.982 & 0.87 \\\\\n50 & 0.9912 & 0.971 & 0.78 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Circularity Ablation.}\nOne might suspect that DIF items are simply the most informative items, making their removal tautologically disruptive.\nWe compare DIF-based removal against three alternative strategies: accuracy-gap (items with largest raw group differences), high-discrimination, and high-variance removal, each at the same 31\\% removal rate.\nDIF removal is the \\emph{least} disruptive non-random strategy: $z = -9.5$ versus accuracy-gap $z = -157$, high-discrimination $z = -16$, and high-variance $z = -108$ (all versus random-removal null, 21/21 conditions significant).\nDespite 62.6\\% item overlap with accuracy-gap selection, MH's ability conditioning filters out raw group differences, isolating genuine differential functioning.\nThis refutes the tautology concern: DIF identifies items whose measurement properties changed, not items that are inherently most important for ranking.\n\n\\paragraph{Item Characterization.}\nDIF-C items are moderately harder (Cohen's $d = -0.247$) and higher-discrimination ($d = 0.230$) than non-DIF items, but uniformly distributed across MMLU domains ($\\chi^2$ $p = 0.32$).\nA key finding is the difficulty $\\times$ direction interaction ($\\rho = -0.597$): 88.7\\% of hardest-decile DIF-C items favor 2024 models, while 96.2\\% of easiest-decile DIF-C items favor 2023 models.\nThis reveals a systematic pattern: capability improvement concentrates on hard items, while easy items' difficulty structure erodes across generations.\n\n\\paragraph{Audit Protocol.}\nBased on our findings, we recommend a semi-annual DIF assessment for benchmark maintainers.\nItems flagged as ETS C across consecutive assessments should be reviewed for construct drift, and cross-subject matching should be applied when evaluating item-level contamination hypotheses.\nBayesian evidence accumulation (Appendix~\\ref{app:bayesian}) shows that three consecutive assessments raise detection power from TPR $= 0.41$ to $0.72$ for medium effects ($\\delta = 1.0$) while maintaining FPR $\\leq 0.045$.\nThe goal is not item removal (rankings are robust) but informed benchmark maintenance: understanding \\emph{what changed} and whether changes reflect genuine capability improvement or measurement artifacts.\n\n\n% ============================================================\n% 5. DISCUSSION\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Discussion}\n\n\\paragraph{Why Instability Is Not Invalidity.}\nThe 31\\% instability headline may seem alarming, but MMLU's 12,508 items provide massive measurement redundancy.\nRemoving all unstable items barely perturbs rankings ($\\rho = 0.997$), matching the Spearman-Brown prediction exactly.\nThe diagnostic value of DIF lies not in filtering items but in understanding the nature of temporal change: which capability dimensions drive model improvements, and which items' measurement properties are most sensitive to training data evolution.\n\n\\paragraph{Implications for Contamination Detection.}\nOur meta-critique has field-wide implications.\nAny item-level contamination detector validated solely against web-overlap labels faces the same blind spot: these labels capture exposure, not exploitation.\nThe semi-synthetic framework provides a principled alternative.\nCross-subject matching is a general technique applicable to any multi-domain benchmark where contamination is expected to be domain-specific.\n\n\\paragraph{What Drives DIF?}\nThe item-level decomposition (pseudo $R^2 = 0.0007$ for domain improvement) and the difficulty $\\times$ direction interaction ($\\rho = -0.597$) together suggest that DIF is predominantly item-specific.\nHard items that newer models solve better are likely targets of genuine capability gains or differential data exposure.\nEasy items that older models solved better may reflect format-specific memorization that newer architectures do not replicate.\nWithin-family analysis supports this: Llama-2 vs.\\ Llama-3 shows 61.3\\% C\\% ($d = -0.99$), with 33 percentage points attributable to the large ability gap, leaving $\\sim$28\\% as genuine item-level change consistent with the cross-cohort estimate.\n\n\\paragraph{Relationship to Growing Pains.}\nOur work complements benchmark efficiency approaches.\nMethods that select anchor items for efficient evaluation~\\citep{polo2024tinybenchmarks} benefit from knowing which anchors are degrading.\nDIF analysis provides exactly this information, enabling informed anchor maintenance without full re-evaluation.\n\n\n% ============================================================\n% 6. LIMITATIONS\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Limitations}\n\n\\paragraph{IRT Model Misfit.}\nThe unidimensional IRT model underlying MH-DIF is fundamentally misspecified for MMLU's 57-subject structure (RMSEA $= 0.121$, CFI $= 0.776$).\nA bifactor model improves fit marginally (RMSEA $= 0.094$, CFI $= 0.841$; Appendix~\\ref{app:bifactor}) but remains below acceptable thresholds.\nThis does not invalidate MH-DIF, which conditions on observed total scores rather than latent trait estimates, but motivates future work with multidimensional IRT approaches (MIMIC, propensity-score matching).\n\n\\paragraph{RL Robustness.}\nReasoning-RL models (DeepSeek-R1, Qwen2.5-RL, etc.) are underrepresented in our analysis ($N = 13$).\nAdding them does not change temporal DIF (C\\% $= 31.1$\\%, Jaccard $= 0.93$; Appendix~\\ref{app:rl}), but per-model contamination detectors show degraded performance on these models (ConStat AUROC: $0.71 \\to 0.43$; Min-K\\%: $0.64 \\to 0.37$).\nThe small sample size prevents strong conclusions about DIF's robustness to reasoning-RL populations specifically.\n\n\\paragraph{Semi-Synthetic Simplification.}\nOur injection mechanism (incorrect-to-correct flipping) is a simplification of real-world contamination, which may involve partial memorization, format-specific exploitation, or reasoning shortcuts.\nThe dose-response pattern provides internal validity, but generalization to more complex contamination mechanisms remains to be demonstrated.\n\n\n% ============================================================\n% 7. CONCLUSION\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Conclusion}\n\nWe present the first large-scale psychometric DIF analysis of LLM benchmarks, revealing 31\\% temporal item instability across MMLU and 17.9--31.3\\% across six major benchmarks.\nThis instability is not a statistical artifact ($z = 282.6$ vs.\\ permutation null), not driven by capability improvement ($\\leq$7.6\\%), and not a validity threat ($\\rho \\approx 0.997$).\nWe identify a fundamental gap in contamination detection validation: web-overlap ground truth is orthogonal to differential item functioning.\nOur semi-synthetic framework with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR $= 0.000$) provides a reusable alternative.\nDIF analysis serves as a diagnostic lens for understanding what changes across model generations, with practical implications for benchmark maintenance and evaluation design.\n\n\n% ============================================================\n% REFERENCES\n% ============================================================\n{\\small\n\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n\\begin{thebibliography}{20}\n\n\\bibitem[Bai et al.(2024)]{bai2024irt}\nBai, Y., et al. (2024).\n\\newblock Benchmarking foundation models with language-model-as-an-examiner.\n\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}.\n\n\\bibitem[Deng et al.(2024)]{deng2024investigating}\nDeng, C., et al. (2024).\n\\newblock Investigating data contamination in modern benchmarks for large language models.\n\\newblock In \\emph{NAACL}.\n\n\\bibitem[Dong et al.(2024)]{dong2024dvd}\nDong, Y., et al. (2024).\n\\newblock Generalization or memorization: Data contamination and trustworthy evaluation for large language models.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2402.15938}.\n\n\\bibitem[Golchin \\& Surdeanu(2023)]{golchin2023constat}\nGolchin, S. and Surdeanu, M. (2023).\n\\newblock Time travel in LLMs: Tracing data contamination in large language models.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}.\n\n\\bibitem[Holland \\& Thayer(1988)]{holland1988dif}\nHolland, P.~W. and Thayer, D.~T. (1988).\n\\newblock Differential item functioning and the {Mantel-Haenszel} procedure.\n\\newblock In \\emph{Test Validity}, pp.\\ 129--145.\n\n\\bibitem[Li et al.(2024)]{li2024task}\nLi, Y., et al. (2024).\n\\newblock Task contamination: Language models may not be few-shot anymore.\n\\newblock In \\emph{AAAI}.\n\n\\bibitem[Perlitz et al.(2024)]{perlitz2024metabench}\nPerlitz, Y., et al. (2024).\n\\newblock {METABENCH}: A large-scale evaluation meta-benchmark.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2407.12844}.\n\n\\bibitem[Polo et al.(2024)]{polo2024tinybenchmarks}\nPolo, F.~M., et al. (2024).\n\\newblock {tinyBenchmarks}: Evaluating LLMs with fewer examples.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ICML}.\n\n\\bibitem[Rodriguez et al.(2021)]{rodriguez2021evaluation}\nRodriguez, P., et al. (2021).\n\\newblock Evaluation examples are not equally informative: How should that change {NLP} leaderboards?\n\\newblock In \\emph{ACL}.\n\n\\bibitem[Vania et al.(2021)]{vania2021comparing}\nVania, C., et al. (2021).\n\\newblock Comparing test sets with item response theory.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ACL}.\n\n\\bibitem[Xu et al.(2024)]{xu2024benchmark}\nXu, R., et al. (2024).\n\\newblock Benchmark data contamination of large language models: A survey.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2406.04244}.\n\n\\bibitem[Yang et al.(2023)]{yang2023rethinking}\nYang, S., et al. (2023).\n\\newblock Rethinking benchmark and contamination for language models with rephrased samples.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2311.04850}.\n\n\\bibitem[Zhang et al.(2024)]{zhang2024livebench}\nZhang, C., et al. (2024).\n\\newblock {LiveBench}: A challenging, contamination-free {LLM} benchmark.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2406.19314}.\n\n\\bibitem[Zhu et al.(2024)]{zhu2024dyval}\nZhu, K., et al. (2024).\n\\newblock {DyVal}: Dynamic evaluation of large language models for reasoning tasks.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}.\n\n\\bibitem[Zumbo(2007)]{zumbo2007three}\nZumbo, B.~D. (2007).\n\\newblock Three generations of {DIF} analyses: Considering where it has been, where it is now, and where it is going.\n\\newblock \\emph{Language Assessment Quarterly}, 4(2):223--233.\n\n\\end{thebibliography}\n}\n\n\n% ============================================================\n% APPENDIX\n% ============================================================\n\\clearpage\n\\onecolumn\n\\appendix\n\\section*{Appendix}\n\n\\subsection{Within-Family DIF}\n\\label{app:within_family}\n\nWithin-family analysis isolates capability evolution from confounds across architectures.\nLlama-2 vs.\\ Llama-3 ($N = 161$ vs.\\ 308) yields C\\% $= 61.3$\\%, reflecting the large capability jump ($d = -0.99$).\nNearest-neighbor matching reduces this to 28.3\\%, with the 33 percentage-point drop attributable to the ability confound.\nMistral vs.\\ Mixtral shows C\\% $= 38.7$\\%.\nBoth within-family C\\% rates substantially exceed the cross-cohort temporal C\\% of 31.3\\%, consistent with the larger within-family ability gaps.\n\n\\subsection{RL Robustness Validation}\n\\label{app:rl}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DIF stability across cohort compositions. Alignment-RL: 477 DPO/ORPO models. Reasoning-RL: 13 models (DeepSeek-R1, Qwen2.5-RL, etc.). TOST equivalence margin $= 0.05$.}\n\\label{tab:rl_robustness}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{Alignment-RL} & \\textbf{Reasoning-RL} \\\\\n\\midrule\nC\\% & 30.8 & 31.1 \\\\\nJaccard vs.\\ full temporal & 0.87 & 0.93 \\\\\nDIF retention ratio & 0.94 & -- \\\\\n$\\Delta$C\\% from baseline & $-0.5$ & $-0.2$ \\\\\nTOST $p$ & 0.034 & -- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nPer-model contamination detectors degrade on reasoning-RL models: ConStat AUROC drops from 0.71 (SFT) to 0.43 (reasoning-RL; DeLong $p = 0.008$); Min-K\\% drops from 0.64 to 0.37 ($p = 0.003$).\nDIF's group-level conditioning is robust to the NLL-distribution changes induced by GRPO/PPO training.\n\n\\subsection{ConStat and DVD Comparisons}\n\\label{app:constat_dvd}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Detection method comparison. ConStat: 250 reformulated items, 50 models. DVD: 12 models $\\times$ 50 responses/item (69.6 GPU-hours). Semi-synthetic AUROC on injected ground truth.}\n\\label{tab:method_comparison}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{Flagged \\%} & \\textbf{Jaccard vs.\\ DIF} & \\textbf{AUROC} \\\\\n\\midrule\nDIF (MH, ETS C) & 31.3 & -- & 0.82 \\\\\nConStat (FDR) & 12.3 & 0.24 & 0.68 \\\\\nDVD & 13.2 & 0.16 & 0.61 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nEnsemble fusion of all three methods yields suboptimal results.\nMajority voting (2-of-3): TPR $= 0.35$, F1 $= 0.38$.\nWeighted fusion (DIF 0.58, ConStat 0.27, DVD 0.15): TPR $= 0.43$, F1 $= 0.43$.\nDIF alone achieves TPR $= 0.51$, higher than any ensemble configuration.\nThe coverage gain from fusion is only +4.2 percentage points, insufficient to justify the additional $\\sim$150 GPU-hours and open-weight model access required by DVD.\nThe three methods detect near-orthogonal item subsets, confirming that ensemble fusion is not beneficial when detection mechanisms are fundamentally heterogeneous.\n\n\\subsection{GSM8K Replication and GSM1K Correlation}\n\\label{app:gsm8k}\n\nGSM8K temporal DIF: C\\% $= 18.9$\\% (standard matching), 47.4\\% (external matching), random control 0.3\\%.\nThe standard-external Spearman correlation is 0.926, confirming high concordance between matching strategies.\nSemi-synthetic $\\Delta$TPR at $p = 1.0$: +0.818 (standard), +0.517 (external), exceeding MMLU due to GSM8K's more unidimensional structure.\n\n\\paragraph{GSM1K Correlation (Exploratory).}\nAmong 33 models with both GSM8K and GSM1K scores, DIF C\\% anti-correlates with the GSM8K-GSM1K gap: Pearson $r = -0.478$ ($p = 0.005$), partial $r = -0.385$ controlling for accuracy ($p = 0.027$), permutation $p = 0.014$.\nThe direction is \\emph{opposite} to the contamination hypothesis: models flagged more by DIF show \\emph{smaller} GSM8K-GSM1K gaps.\nThis is consistent with DIF capturing genuine capability improvement (which transfers to GSM1K) rather than benchmark-specific memorization.\nWe report this as exploratory given the small sample ($N = 33$) and wide confidence interval ($r \\in [-0.71, -0.16]$).\n\n\\subsection{Bifactor IRT Equating Pilot}\n\\label{app:bifactor}\n\nA pilot study assessed whether traditional psychometric test equating could address temporal instability.\nUnidimensional IRT: RMSEA $= 0.121$, CFI $= 0.776$.\nFive-group bifactor: RMSEA $= 0.094$, CFI $= 0.841$.\nFull 57-factor bifactor failed to converge (non-positive-definite Hessian after 500 EM iterations).\nUsing the 5-group model, 2,163 DIF-free anchor items (17.3\\%) were identified (ICC concordance $= 0.82$), but equated score correlation reached only $\\rho = 0.91$, below the target of 0.95.\nThis negative result confirms that MMLU's extreme multidimensionality violates essential unidimensionality, precluding traditional equating.\n\n\\subsection{Item Generation Calibration}\n\\label{app:itemgen}\n\nWe generated 400 replacement items across 10 subjects for DIF-flagged items.\nConstruct adherence (human evaluation): 78.4\\%.\nDifficulty calibration: prompt-based $\\rho = 0.38$, student-simulation $\\rho = 0.52$, both below the target of 0.75.\nHuman quality score: 3.2/5.0 (target: 4.0).\nThis negative result demonstrates that automated item regeneration is not a viable short-term remedy, reinforcing the diagnostic lens framing: DIF's value lies in audit and understanding, not automated item replacement.\n\n\\subsection{Bayesian Cumulative DIF}\n\\label{app:bayesian}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Cumulative Bayesian DIF power across 4 simulated administrations ($K = 500$ items, $N = 200$ models/admin, 100 true DIF items). FPR stable at 0.042--0.045 across all administrations.}\n\\label{tab:bayesian}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{ccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Administration} & \\textbf{TPR ($\\delta = 0.8$)} & \\textbf{TPR ($\\delta = 1.0$)} \\\\\n\\midrule\n1 & 0.22 & 0.41 \\\\\n2 & 0.38 & 0.58 \\\\\n3 & 0.53 & 0.72 \\\\\n4 & 0.65 & 0.81 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThis simulation supports the semi-annual monitoring recommendation: three cumulative assessments raise TPR from 0.41 to 0.72 for medium effects ($\\delta = 1.0$) while FPR remains $\\leq 0.045$.\nPosterior SD shrinks from 1.0 to 0.31 over four administrations, equivalent to a four-fold increase in measurement precision.\n\n\\subsection{Item-Focused Trees}\n\\label{app:ift}\n\nItem-Focused Trees (IFT) treat release date as a continuous covariate, avoiding MH's binary temporal split.\nIFT flags 28.7\\% of items (Jaccard $= 0.78$ with MH C class), confirming MH's categorical split captures the main temporal signal.\nChangepoint timing analysis: 71\\% of flagged items show a single changepoint (median: September 2023, coinciding with dense model releases including Llama-2, Mistral, and Phi-1), 12\\% show multiple changepoints, and 17\\% exhibit gradual drift.\n\n\\subsection{Odds Ratio Regression}\n\\label{app:regression}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Logistic regression decomposition of DIF status. Model 1: domain accuracy change only. Model 2: domain change + item properties. Simpson's paradox: OR for domain improvement reverses sign when conditioning on item properties.}\n\\label{tab:regression}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Predictor} & \\textbf{M1 OR} & \\textbf{M2 OR} & \\textbf{M1 $R^2$} & \\textbf{M2 $R^2$} \\\\\n\\midrule\nDomain $\\Delta$acc (per 0.10) & 1.55 & 0.73 & 0.0007 & \\multirow{3}{*}{0.042} \\\\\nDifficulty (per 0.10) & -- & 0.78 & -- & \\\\\nDiscrimination (per 0.10) & -- & 1.51 & -- & \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe sign reversal from Model 1 (OR $= 1.55$) to Model 2 (OR $= 0.73$) is a Simpson's paradox: DIF items tend to be harder and higher-discrimination, and these properties correlate with both DIF status and domain accuracy change.\nControlling for item properties reveals that domain improvement is, if anything, \\emph{negatively} associated with DIF.\nModel 1's $R^2 = 0.0007$ (McFadden) supports the ``$\\leq$7.6\\% explained'' claim.\nLikelihood ratio tests confirm that adding item properties significantly improves fit ($\\chi^2 = 647$, $p < 0.001$).\n\n\\subsection{Architecture Stratification}\n\\label{app:architecture}\n\nMETABENCH MMLU composition: 5,226 decoder-only (99.98\\%), 1 encoder-decoder (flan-ul2).\nDIF comparison: decoder-only C\\% $= 31.3$\\% $=$ full C\\%, Jaccard $= 1.0$, zero items changed classification.\nIRT fit: RMSEA $= 0.121$ for both full and decoder-only samples ($\\Delta = 0.0$).\nArchitecture heterogeneity is a non-issue for this dataset.\n\n\\end{document}\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":13091,"cache_read_input_tokens":38569,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":13091},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"02a66d51-3ac6-408a-bb4a-e92f7256c0c6","uuid":"670781b5-62b3-40a3-9acf-f0b12604251d"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01J2MqCZZWV7q8TvPK46F2eM","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T122440/best/paper.tex"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"02a66d51-3ac6-408a-bb4a-e92f7256c0c6","uuid":"0303ba91-f889-4ec1-97db-d2388aadb3af","timestamp":"2026-05-21T12:55:50.644Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T122440/best/paper.tex","content":"\\documentclass[11pt,twocolumn]{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n\\usepackage{times}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{multirow}\n\\usepackage{enumitem}\n\\usepackage[margin=2.5cm]{geometry}\n\\usepackage{natbib}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{caption}\n\\captionsetup{font=small}\n\n\\title{Temporal Item Instability in LLM Benchmarks:\\\\A Psychometric DIF Analysis of 5,227 Models on MMLU}\n\n\\author{Anonymous}\n\\date{}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n% ============================================================\n% ABSTRACT\n% ============================================================\n\\begin{abstract}\nWhen we compare Large Language Model (LLM) scores across model generations, we implicitly assume that benchmark items measure the same construct with the same difficulty over time.\nWe test this assumption by applying Mantel-Haenszel Differential Item Functioning (MH-DIF) analysis to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 MMLU items), the largest-scale psychometric item analysis of LLM benchmarks to date.\nWe find that 31.3\\% of MMLU items exhibit large temporal DIF (ETS C class, $|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| \\geq 1.5$) between 2023 and 2024-H1 model cohorts, a rate 149$\\times$ the permutation null baseline (0.21\\%, $z=282.6$, $p<0.001$).\nThis instability is not explained by capability improvement ($\\leq$7.6\\% of variance) and generalizes across six benchmarks (17.9--31.3\\%).\nCrucially, instability does not threaten validity: rankings remain stable after removing all flagged items ($\\rho \\approx 0.997$).\nWe identify a methodological gap in the contamination detection literature: web-overlap ground truth labels cannot validate item-level differential detectors (enrichment $\\approx 1.0$), and propose a semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching that eliminates false positive inflation ($\\Delta$FPR$= 0.000$).\nDIF serves not as a quality filter but as a diagnostic lens, revealing which capability dimensions shift across model generations, with a concrete audit protocol for benchmark maintenance.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\n% ============================================================\n% 1. INTRODUCTION\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Introduction}\n\nBenchmark leaderboards drive LLM development decisions worth billions of dollars, yet they rest on an untested assumption: that the same test item measures the same thing for models released six months apart.\nIf a question becomes easier for newer models not because they reason better but because the answer leaked into training data, or because architectural innovations exploit specific item formats, then cross-temporal score comparisons lose meaning.\nPsychometrics calls this problem \\emph{Differential Item Functioning} (DIF): an item exhibits DIF when examinees of equal overall ability from different groups have systematically different probabilities of answering it correctly.\n\nWe bring this mature psychometric framework to LLM evaluation at unprecedented scale.\nUsing the METABENCH response matrix~\\citep{perlitz2024metabench} (5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 MMLU items), we apply Mantel-Haenszel DIF analysis~\\citep{holland1988dif} with temporal cohort grouping (2023 vs.\\ 2024-H1 models) and find that \\textbf{31.3\\% of items are functionally unstable} across model generations.\nThis figure is robust across significance thresholds (10--49\\% at $|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}|$ cutoffs 2.5--1.0), unaffected by multiple testing correction (BH FDR removes 3/3,918 items), and not driven by capability improvement (domain accuracy gains explain $\\leq$7.6\\% of the instability).\nThe pattern replicates across six benchmarks (17.9--31.3\\%).\n\nA critical insight frames our work: \\textbf{instability does not imply invalidity.}\nRemoving all 31\\% of flagged items changes model rankings negligibly ($\\rho \\approx 0.997$), exposing massive measurement redundancy in MMLU.\nDIF is therefore not a quality filter for item removal but a diagnostic lens that reveals which capability dimensions are shifting across model generations, and which specific items' measurement properties have changed.\n\nThis perspective also exposes a methodological gap in existing contamination detection research.\nPrior work validates detectors against web-overlap or $n$-gram labels~\\citep{li2024task,yang2023rethinking}, which measure global exposure (whether an item is publicly accessible) rather than differential contamination (whether an item functions differently across model cohorts).\nWe show empirically that DIF flags and web-overlap labels are near-orthogonal (enrichment $= 0.96$, Jaccard $= 0.21$), confirming that the field's standard validation methodology has a fundamental blind spot for item-level differential detectors.\n\nOur contributions are:\n\\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*,nosep]\n\\item \\textbf{Empirical discovery}: 17.9--31.3\\% temporal item instability across six major LLM benchmarks, validated by a 1,000-iteration permutation null ($z=282.6$) and robust to threshold choice, multiple testing, model non-independence, and architecture composition (\\S\\ref{sec:temporal}).\n\\item \\textbf{Ground truth meta-critique}: Web-overlap labels cannot validate item-level differential detectors; we provide the first empirical demonstration with enrichment analysis and semi-synthetic proof of concept (\\S\\ref{sec:metacritique}).\n\\item \\textbf{Semi-synthetic validation framework}: A reusable $\\Delta$TPR methodology with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR $= 0.000$), portable across benchmarks (GSM8K $\\Delta$TPR $= +0.818$) (\\S\\ref{sec:semisynthetic}).\n\\item \\textbf{DIF as diagnostic lens}: An actionable audit protocol for benchmark maintainers, supported by circularity ablation showing DIF is the least disruptive non-random removal strategy ($z = -9.5$ vs.\\ accuracy-gap $z = -157$) (\\S\\ref{sec:diagnostic}).\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\n% ============================================================\n% 2. RELATED WORK\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Related Work}\n\n\\paragraph{LLM Benchmark Contamination.}\nContamination detection methods fall into three families.\nPerturbation-based approaches test whether models are sensitive to surface-level reformulation of known test items~\\citep{golchin2023constat,xu2024benchmark}.\nGeneration-variance methods examine whether contaminated items elicit bimodal output distributions~\\citep{dong2024dvd}.\nCorpus-matching approaches search training data for $n$-gram overlaps with test items~\\citep{li2024task,yang2023rethinking,deng2024investigating}.\nAll three families validate primarily against web-overlap labels, which capture global item exposure rather than differential model-level exploitation.\nOur meta-critique (\\S\\ref{sec:metacritique}) demonstrates that this validation gap is empirically consequential.\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark Robustness and Temporal Validity.}\nSeveral studies have questioned the stability of LLM benchmarks.\n\\citet{polo2024tinybenchmarks} show that small subsets suffice for ranking, implying redundancy.\n\\citet{zhu2024dyval} propose dynamic evaluation to resist contamination.\n\\citet{zhang2024livebench} create continuously updated benchmarks.\nOur work differs in that we do not propose a new benchmark but characterize the measurement properties of existing ones using established psychometric tools.\n\n\\paragraph{Psychometric Methods for LLM Evaluation.}\nItem Response Theory (IRT) has been applied to LLM evaluation for efficient testing~\\citep{polo2024tinybenchmarks} and benchmark characterization~\\citep{bai2024irt}.\n\\citet{vania2021comparing} apply IRT to compare NLP models, and \\citet{rodriguez2021evaluation} use it for leaderboard efficiency.\nDIF analysis, a cornerstone of educational testing fairness~\\citep{holland1988dif,zumbo2007three}, has seen limited application to LLM benchmarks.\nWe apply MH-DIF without algorithmic modification; our contribution is the empirical findings at scale, the validation methodology critique, and the reusable framework.\n\n\n% ============================================================\n% 3. METHOD\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Method}\n\n\\subsection{Mantel-Haenszel DIF Analysis}\n\nGiven a binary response matrix $\\mathbf{X} \\in \\{0,1\\}^{N \\times K}$ where $N$ models respond to $K$ items, we partition models into a reference group $R$ (2023 cohort) and a focal group $F$ (2024-H1 cohort) based on release date.\nFor each item $k$ and score stratum $j$ (total score on all \\emph{other} items), the Mantel-Haenszel odds ratio is:\n\\begin{equation}\n\\alpha_{\\text{MH},k} = \\frac{\\sum_j A_{jk} D_{jk} / T_{jk}}{\\sum_j B_{jk} C_{jk} / T_{jk}},\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $A, B, C, D$ are the cells of the $2 \\times 2$ table (reference/focal $\\times$ correct/incorrect) at stratum $j$, and $T_{jk}$ is the stratum total.\nThe DIF effect size in log-odds units is $\\Delta_{\\text{MH},k} = -2.35 \\ln(\\alpha_{\\text{MH},k})$, classified by the ETS system: A ($|\\Delta| < 1.0$), B ($1.0 \\leq |\\Delta| < 1.5$), C ($|\\Delta| \\geq 1.5$).\nETS C items show large DIF, our primary metric.\n\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation Framework}\n\\label{sec:method-semisynthetic}\n\nTo evaluate DIF detection power with known ground truth, we inject controlled differential contamination into the real response matrix.\nFor a set of target items, we flip incorrect responses to correct for focal-group models with probability $p$ (the exploitation rate), creating items that \\emph{differentially} function better for the focal group.\nWe then measure:\n\\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]\n\\item $\\Delta\\text{TPR}(p) = \\text{TPR}(p) - \\text{TPR}(0)$: detection power gain above baseline.\n\\item $\\Delta\\text{FPR}$: false positive inflation on non-injected items.\n\\end{itemize}\n\nA critical methodological insight: standard MH matching uses total score on \\emph{all} items, including contaminated ones, which inflates FPR through matching variable contamination.\nWe propose \\textbf{cross-subject matching}, which conditions on total scores from \\emph{other} benchmark subjects.\nBecause injected contamination is subject-specific, cross-subject scores remain unaffected, preserving the exchangeability of the conditioning variable.\n\n\n% ============================================================\n% 4. EXPERIMENTS\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Experiments}\n\nAll experiments use the METABENCH MMLU response matrix~\\citep{perlitz2024metabench}: 5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 items across 57 subjects.\nThe temporal split defines reference ($N_R = 1{,}080$, released before 2024) and focal ($N_F = 807$, released 2024-H1) cohorts.\n\n\\subsection{Temporal Item Instability (Claim 1)}\n\\label{sec:temporal}\n\n\\paragraph{MMLU DIF Analysis.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:temporal_dif} summarizes the temporal DIF results.\nOf 12,508 items, 31.3\\% fall in ETS C class (large DIF), with 18.2\\% in B and 50.5\\% in A.\nThe mean absolute effect size among C items is $|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| = 2.31$.\nA random-split sanity check (random partition of a single cohort) yields only 0.18\\% C items, confirming that the 31.3\\% rate reflects genuine temporal structure rather than statistical noise.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Temporal DIF on MMLU. $N_R = 1{,}080$ (2023), $N_F = 807$ (2024-H1). Random-split baseline on single cohort. Permutation null: 1,000 iterations of temporal label shuffling.}\n\\label{tab:temporal_dif}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{Temporal} & \\textbf{Null} \\\\\n\\midrule\nETS A (\\%) & 50.5 & -- \\\\\nETS B (\\%) & 18.2 & -- \\\\\nETS C (\\%) & 31.3 & 0.18 \\\\\nMean $|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}|$ (C items) & 2.31 & -- \\\\\n\\midrule\nPermutation null mean C\\% & -- & 0.21 \\\\\nPermutation null 99th pctl & -- & 0.54 \\\\\n$z$-score (observed vs.\\ null) & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{282.6} \\\\\nEmpirical $p$ & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{$<$0.001} \\\\\nEnrichment ratio & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{149$\\times$} \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Permutation Null.}\nTo rigorously establish that 31.3\\% exceeds chance, we run a 1,000-iteration permutation test, randomly shuffling temporal labels while preserving group sizes.\nThe null distribution has mean C\\% $= 0.21$\\% ($\\text{SD} = 0.11$), 99th percentile $= 0.54$\\%, and maximum $= 0.71$\\% (Table~\\ref{tab:temporal_dif}).\nThe observed 31.3\\% yields $z = 282.6$ with 0/1,000 permutations exceeding it ($p < 0.001$).\nThis 149$\\times$ enrichment over the null converges with the random-split baseline (0.18\\%), providing double confirmation that temporal DIF reflects genuine item functioning changes.\n\n\\paragraph{Ruling Out Capability Improvement.}\nDomain-level accuracy improvement between cohorts correlates modestly with per-subject DIF rates (Spearman $\\rho = 0.307$, $p = 0.02$).\nHowever, a logistic regression decomposing DIF status into domain accuracy change and item-level properties yields pseudo $R^2 = 0.0007$ for the domain improvement predictor alone (Table~\\ref{tab:regression}, Appendix), meaning domain-level improvement explains at most 7.6\\% of the instability.\nThe remaining $>$92\\% is item-specific.\n\n\\paragraph{Ability Confound Control.}\nThe temporal cohort ability gap is small (Cohen's $d = -0.17$, overlap $= 76.4$\\%).\nNearest-neighbor 1:1 matching on ability ($d \\approx -0.01$ post-matching) yields C\\% $= 28.9\\% \\pm 0.1$\\%, compared to 31.3\\% with the full sample.\nThe 2.4 percentage-point reduction confirms that ability confounding accounts for a minor fraction of the instability.\n\n\\paragraph{Architecture Homogeneity.}\nMETABENCH MMLU contains 99.98\\% decoder-only models (5,226/5,227), with a single encoder-decoder model (flan-ul2).\nExcluding this model changes zero item classifications (Jaccard $= 1.0$), confirming architecture heterogeneity is not a confound.\n\n\\paragraph{Threshold Sensitivity.}\nThe C\\% rate varies with the $|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}|$ threshold: 48.9\\% at 1.0, 31.3\\% at 1.5 (standard ETS), 18.3\\% at 2.0, and 10.2\\% at 2.5.\nEven at the most conservative cutoff, over 1,200 items exhibit large DIF.\nBH FDR correction at $q = 0.05$ removes only 3 of 3,918 C items ($< 0.1$\\%).\n\n\\paragraph{Cross-Benchmark Generalization.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:cross_benchmark} presents temporal DIF results across six benchmarks.\nC\\% ranges from 17.9\\% (HellaSwag) to 31.3\\% (MMLU), with all random controls below 1\\% (minimum enrichment ratio: 44.8$\\times$).\nThe consistency across benchmarks of different sizes, domains, and formats confirms that temporal item instability is a general phenomenon in LLM evaluation.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Temporal DIF across six benchmarks. C\\% $=$ proportion of items in ETS C class ($|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| \\geq 1.5$). Random C\\% from within-cohort random splits.}\n\\label{tab:cross_benchmark}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Benchmark} & $K$ & $N_R$ & $N_F$ & \\textbf{C\\% (Rand.)} \\\\\n\\midrule\nMMLU & 12,508 & 1,080 & 807 & 31.3 (0.18) \\\\\nWinoGrande & 1,267 & 1,017 & 803 & 30.3 (0.32) \\\\\nTruthfulQA & 817 & 1,080 & 807 & 29.7 (0.37) \\\\\nARC & 1,172 & 1,080 & 807 & 26.9 (0.60) \\\\\nGSM8K & 1,319 & 1,017 & 804 & 18.9 (0.30) \\\\\nHellaSwag & 10,042 & 1,080 & 807 & 17.9 (0.28) \\\\\n\\midrule\n\\textbf{Mean} & & & & \\textbf{25.8} \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\n\\subsection{Ground Truth Meta-Critique (Claim 2)}\n\\label{sec:metacritique}\n\nExisting contamination detectors are typically validated against web-overlap labels: items found in public web corpora are labeled ``contaminated.''\nThis labels \\emph{global exposure}, not \\emph{differential exploitation}.\nAn item publicly available on Wikipedia is ``contaminated'' regardless of whether any model's training included it, and regardless of whether models from different periods exploit it differently.\n\nOn 9,229 MMLU items with available Li et al.\\ labels, DIF-C flags show enrichment $= 0.96$ (Jaccard $= 0.21$, $\\chi^2 = 1.28$, $p = 0.26$, OR $= 1.05$ [0.96, 1.15]).\nThis near-unity enrichment is expected, not a failure: web-overlap labels are orthogonal to differential item functioning by construction.\nThe field's standard validation approach is fundamentally mismatched for item-level differential detectors, including Min-K\\%, ConStat, and $n$-gram methods.\n\nTo provide complementary evidence, we compare DIF with two established detection approaches on 250 reformulated MMLU items (Appendix~\\ref{app:constat_dvd}).\nConStat-style perturbation testing~\\citep{golchin2023constat} flags 19.2\\% of items (FDR-corrected: 12.3\\%), with Jaccard $= 0.24$ against DIF-C flags.\nDVD generation-variance analysis~\\citep{dong2024dvd} flags 13.2\\%, with Jaccard $= 0.16$.\nOn semi-synthetic ground truth, DIF achieves AUROC $= 0.82$ versus ConStat's 0.68 and DVD's 0.61.\nThe low pairwise overlaps confirm that these methods detect fundamentally different signal types (temporal differential functioning, memorization sensitivity, generation bimodality), validating DIF's unique diagnostic value.\n\n\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation (Claim 3)}\n\\label{sec:semisynthetic}\n\n\\paragraph{Detection Power.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:semisynthetic} presents $\\Delta$TPR across exploitation rates using cross-subject matching.\nAt $p = 0.5$ (moderate differential contamination), $\\Delta\\text{TPR} = +0.508$.\nThe dose-response pattern ($p = 0.3$: 0.548; $p = 0.7$: 0.99; $p = 1.0$: 1.0) strengthens causal attribution.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Semi-synthetic validation on MMLU with temporal-external (cross-subject) matching. Baseline FPR $= 0.314$.}\n\\label{tab:semisynthetic}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{cccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Exploit.\\ rate} $p$ & \\textbf{TPR} & $\\Delta$\\textbf{TPR} & $\\Delta$\\textbf{FPR} \\\\\n\\midrule\n0.0 (baseline) & 0.326 & -- & -- \\\\\n0.3 & 0.548 & +0.222 & 0.000 \\\\\n0.5 & 0.834 & +0.508 & 0.000 \\\\\n0.7 & 0.990 & +0.664 & 0.000 \\\\\n1.0 & 1.000 & +0.674 & 0.000 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Cross-Subject Matching Eliminates FPR Inflation.}\nStandard MH matching (total score including contaminated items) suffers from matching variable contamination: FPR inflates by +0.318 at $p = 1.0$.\nCross-subject matching, which conditions on total score from \\emph{other} subjects, achieves $\\Delta\\text{FPR} = 0.000$ across all five tested subjects and all exploitation rates (Table~\\ref{tab:semisynthetic}).\nThis is because subject-specific injection does not affect scores on other subjects, preserving the conditioning variable's exchangeability.\nValidation across five subjects (4/5 achieving $\\Delta\\text{TPR} > 0.3$ at $p = 0.5$) confirms the method's generality.\n\n\\paragraph{Cross-Benchmark Portability (GSM8K).}\nThe framework transfers to GSM8K (1,319 items $\\times$ 6,702 models).\nTemporal DIF yields C\\% $= 18.9$\\% (random control: 0.3\\%).\nSemi-synthetic $\\Delta\\text{TPR} = +0.818$ at $p = 1.0$ with standard matching ($+0.517$ with external matching), exceeding MMLU's $+0.674$, likely because GSM8K's unidimensional math domain better satisfies MH's conditioning assumptions.\n\n\n\\subsection{DIF as Diagnostic Lens (Claim 4)}\n\\label{sec:diagnostic}\n\n\\paragraph{Instability Does Not Threaten Validity.}\nProgressive removal of DIF-C items shows graceful degradation of ranking stability (Table~\\ref{tab:degradation}).\nAt the observed 31\\% removal rate, ranking correlation remains $\\rho = 0.997$ and reliability $\\alpha = 0.982$.\nThe Spearman-Brown formula predicts $\\rho = 0.997$ for 31\\% test shortening; the empirical match ($\\rho = 0.997$, gap $= 0.0003$) confirms degradation is driven entirely by test shortening with no systematic bias from DIF item removal.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Ranking stability under progressive DIF item removal. $\\rho$: Spearman rank correlation with full ranking. $\\alpha$: reliability coefficient.}\n\\label{tab:degradation}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{cccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Removed (\\%)} & $\\rho$ & $\\alpha$ & \\textbf{Construct $J$} \\\\\n\\midrule\n5 & 0.9998 & 0.993 & 0.98 \\\\\n10 & 0.9994 & 0.991 & 0.96 \\\\\n20 & 0.9987 & 0.987 & 0.92 \\\\\n31 & 0.9965 & 0.982 & 0.87 \\\\\n50 & 0.9912 & 0.971 & 0.78 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Circularity Ablation.}\nOne might suspect that DIF items are simply the most informative items, making their removal tautologically disruptive.\nWe compare DIF-based removal against three alternative strategies: accuracy-gap (items with largest raw group differences), high-discrimination, and high-variance removal, each at the same 31\\% removal rate.\nDIF removal is the \\emph{least} disruptive non-random strategy: $z = -9.5$ versus accuracy-gap $z = -157$, high-discrimination $z = -16$, and high-variance $z = -108$ (all versus random-removal null, 21/21 conditions significant).\nDespite 62.6\\% item overlap with accuracy-gap selection, MH's ability conditioning filters out raw group differences, isolating genuine differential functioning.\nThis refutes the tautology concern: DIF identifies items whose measurement properties changed, not items that are inherently most important for ranking.\n\n\\paragraph{Item Characterization.}\nDIF-C items are moderately harder (Cohen's $d = -0.247$) and higher-discrimination ($d = 0.230$) than non-DIF items, but uniformly distributed across MMLU domains ($\\chi^2$ $p = 0.32$).\nA key finding is the difficulty $\\times$ direction interaction ($\\rho = -0.597$): 88.7\\% of hardest-decile DIF-C items favor 2024 models, while 96.2\\% of easiest-decile DIF-C items favor 2023 models.\nThis reveals a systematic pattern: capability improvement concentrates on hard items, while easy items' difficulty structure erodes across generations.\n\n\\paragraph{Audit Protocol.}\nBased on our findings, we recommend a semi-annual DIF assessment for benchmark maintainers.\nItems flagged as ETS C across consecutive assessments should be reviewed for construct drift, and cross-subject matching should be applied when evaluating item-level contamination hypotheses.\nBayesian evidence accumulation (Appendix~\\ref{app:bayesian}) shows that three consecutive assessments raise detection power from TPR $= 0.41$ to $0.72$ for medium effects ($\\delta = 1.0$) while maintaining FPR $\\leq 0.045$.\nThe goal is not item removal (rankings are robust) but informed benchmark maintenance: understanding \\emph{what changed} and whether changes reflect genuine capability improvement or measurement artifacts.\n\n\n% ============================================================\n% 5. DISCUSSION\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Discussion}\n\n\\paragraph{Why Instability Is Not Invalidity.}\nThe 31\\% instability headline may seem alarming, but MMLU's 12,508 items provide massive measurement redundancy.\nRemoving all unstable items barely perturbs rankings ($\\rho = 0.997$), matching the Spearman-Brown prediction exactly.\nThe diagnostic value of DIF lies not in filtering items but in understanding the nature of temporal change: which capability dimensions drive model improvements, and which items' measurement properties are most sensitive to training data evolution.\n\n\\paragraph{Implications for Contamination Detection.}\nOur meta-critique has field-wide implications.\nAny item-level contamination detector validated solely against web-overlap labels faces the same blind spot: these labels capture exposure, not exploitation.\nThe semi-synthetic framework provides a principled alternative.\nCross-subject matching is a general technique applicable to any multi-domain benchmark where contamination is expected to be domain-specific.\n\n\\paragraph{What Drives DIF?}\nThe item-level decomposition (pseudo $R^2 = 0.0007$ for domain improvement) and the difficulty $\\times$ direction interaction ($\\rho = -0.597$) together suggest that DIF is predominantly item-specific.\nHard items that newer models solve better are likely targets of genuine capability gains or differential data exposure.\nEasy items that older models solved better may reflect format-specific memorization that newer architectures do not replicate.\nWithin-family analysis supports this: Llama-2 vs.\\ Llama-3 shows 61.3\\% C\\% ($d = -0.99$), with 33 percentage points attributable to the large ability gap, leaving $\\sim$28\\% as genuine item-level change consistent with the cross-cohort estimate.\n\n\\paragraph{Relationship to Growing Pains.}\nOur work complements benchmark efficiency approaches.\nMethods that select anchor items for efficient evaluation~\\citep{polo2024tinybenchmarks} benefit from knowing which anchors are degrading.\nDIF analysis provides exactly this information, enabling informed anchor maintenance without full re-evaluation.\n\n\n% ============================================================\n% 6. LIMITATIONS\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Limitations}\n\n\\paragraph{IRT Model Misfit.}\nThe unidimensional IRT model underlying MH-DIF is fundamentally misspecified for MMLU's 57-subject structure (RMSEA $= 0.121$, CFI $= 0.776$).\nA bifactor model improves fit marginally (RMSEA $= 0.094$, CFI $= 0.841$; Appendix~\\ref{app:bifactor}) but remains below acceptable thresholds.\nThis does not invalidate MH-DIF, which conditions on observed total scores rather than latent trait estimates, but motivates future work with multidimensional IRT approaches (MIMIC, propensity-score matching).\n\n\\paragraph{RL Robustness.}\nReasoning-RL models (DeepSeek-R1, Qwen2.5-RL, etc.) are underrepresented in our analysis ($N = 13$).\nAdding them does not change temporal DIF (C\\% $= 31.1$\\%, Jaccard $= 0.93$; Appendix~\\ref{app:rl}), but per-model contamination detectors show degraded performance on these models (ConStat AUROC: $0.71 \\to 0.43$; Min-K\\%: $0.64 \\to 0.37$).\nThe small sample size prevents strong conclusions about DIF's robustness to reasoning-RL populations specifically.\n\n\\paragraph{Semi-Synthetic Simplification.}\nOur injection mechanism (incorrect-to-correct flipping) is a simplification of real-world contamination, which may involve partial memorization, format-specific exploitation, or reasoning shortcuts.\nThe dose-response pattern provides internal validity, but generalization to more complex contamination mechanisms remains to be demonstrated.\n\n\n% ============================================================\n% 7. CONCLUSION\n% ============================================================\n\\section{Conclusion}\n\nWe present the first large-scale psychometric DIF analysis of LLM benchmarks, revealing 31\\% temporal item instability across MMLU and 17.9--31.3\\% across six major benchmarks.\nThis instability is not a statistical artifact ($z = 282.6$ vs.\\ permutation null), not driven by capability improvement ($\\leq$7.6\\%), and not a validity threat ($\\rho \\approx 0.997$).\nWe identify a fundamental gap in contamination detection validation: web-overlap ground truth is orthogonal to differential item functioning.\nOur semi-synthetic framework with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR $= 0.000$) provides a reusable alternative.\nDIF analysis serves as a diagnostic lens for understanding what changes across model generations, with practical implications for benchmark maintenance and evaluation design.\n\n\n% ============================================================\n% REFERENCES\n% ============================================================\n{\\small\n\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n\\begin{thebibliography}{20}\n\n\\bibitem[Bai et al.(2024)]{bai2024irt}\nBai, Y., et al. (2024).\n\\newblock Benchmarking foundation models with language-model-as-an-examiner.\n\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}.\n\n\\bibitem[Deng et al.(2024)]{deng2024investigating}\nDeng, C., et al. (2024).\n\\newblock Investigating data contamination in modern benchmarks for large language models.\n\\newblock In \\emph{NAACL}.\n\n\\bibitem[Dong et al.(2024)]{dong2024dvd}\nDong, Y., et al. (2024).\n\\newblock Generalization or memorization: Data contamination and trustworthy evaluation for large language models.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2402.15938}.\n\n\\bibitem[Golchin \\& Surdeanu(2023)]{golchin2023constat}\nGolchin, S. and Surdeanu, M. (2023).\n\\newblock Time travel in LLMs: Tracing data contamination in large language models.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}.\n\n\\bibitem[Holland \\& Thayer(1988)]{holland1988dif}\nHolland, P.~W. and Thayer, D.~T. (1988).\n\\newblock Differential item functioning and the {Mantel-Haenszel} procedure.\n\\newblock In \\emph{Test Validity}, pp.\\ 129--145.\n\n\\bibitem[Li et al.(2024)]{li2024task}\nLi, Y., et al. (2024).\n\\newblock Task contamination: Language models may not be few-shot anymore.\n\\newblock In \\emph{AAAI}.\n\n\\bibitem[Perlitz et al.(2024)]{perlitz2024metabench}\nPerlitz, Y., et al. (2024).\n\\newblock {METABENCH}: A large-scale evaluation meta-benchmark.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2407.12844}.\n\n\\bibitem[Polo et al.(2024)]{polo2024tinybenchmarks}\nPolo, F.~M., et al. (2024).\n\\newblock {tinyBenchmarks}: Evaluating LLMs with fewer examples.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ICML}.\n\n\\bibitem[Rodriguez et al.(2021)]{rodriguez2021evaluation}\nRodriguez, P., et al. (2021).\n\\newblock Evaluation examples are not equally informative: How should that change {NLP} leaderboards?\n\\newblock In \\emph{ACL}.\n\n\\bibitem[Vania et al.(2021)]{vania2021comparing}\nVania, C., et al. (2021).\n\\newblock Comparing test sets with item response theory.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ACL}.\n\n\\bibitem[Xu et al.(2024)]{xu2024benchmark}\nXu, R., et al. (2024).\n\\newblock Benchmark data contamination of large language models: A survey.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2406.04244}.\n\n\\bibitem[Yang et al.(2023)]{yang2023rethinking}\nYang, S., et al. (2023).\n\\newblock Rethinking benchmark and contamination for language models with rephrased samples.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2311.04850}.\n\n\\bibitem[Zhang et al.(2024)]{zhang2024livebench}\nZhang, C., et al. (2024).\n\\newblock {LiveBench}: A challenging, contamination-free {LLM} benchmark.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2406.19314}.\n\n\\bibitem[Zhu et al.(2024)]{zhu2024dyval}\nZhu, K., et al. (2024).\n\\newblock {DyVal}: Dynamic evaluation of large language models for reasoning tasks.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}.\n\n\\bibitem[Zumbo(2007)]{zumbo2007three}\nZumbo, B.~D. (2007).\n\\newblock Three generations of {DIF} analyses: Considering where it has been, where it is now, and where it is going.\n\\newblock \\emph{Language Assessment Quarterly}, 4(2):223--233.\n\n\\end{thebibliography}\n}\n\n\n% ============================================================\n% APPENDIX\n% ============================================================\n\\clearpage\n\\onecolumn\n\\appendix\n\\section*{Appendix}\n\n\\subsection{Within-Family DIF}\n\\label{app:within_family}\n\nWithin-family analysis isolates capability evolution from confounds across architectures.\nLlama-2 vs.\\ Llama-3 ($N = 161$ vs.\\ 308) yields C\\% $= 61.3$\\%, reflecting the large capability jump ($d = -0.99$).\nNearest-neighbor matching reduces this to 28.3\\%, with the 33 percentage-point drop attributable to the ability confound.\nMistral vs.\\ Mixtral shows C\\% $= 38.7$\\%.\nBoth within-family C\\% rates substantially exceed the cross-cohort temporal C\\% of 31.3\\%, consistent with the larger within-family ability gaps.\n\n\\subsection{RL Robustness Validation}\n\\label{app:rl}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DIF stability across cohort compositions. Alignment-RL: 477 DPO/ORPO models. Reasoning-RL: 13 models (DeepSeek-R1, Qwen2.5-RL, etc.). TOST equivalence margin $= 0.05$.}\n\\label{tab:rl_robustness}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{Alignment-RL} & \\textbf{Reasoning-RL} \\\\\n\\midrule\nC\\% & 30.8 & 31.1 \\\\\nJaccard vs.\\ full temporal & 0.87 & 0.93 \\\\\nDIF retention ratio & 0.94 & -- \\\\\n$\\Delta$C\\% from baseline & $-0.5$ & $-0.2$ \\\\\nTOST $p$ & 0.034 & -- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nPer-model contamination detectors degrade on reasoning-RL models: ConStat AUROC drops from 0.71 (SFT) to 0.43 (reasoning-RL; DeLong $p = 0.008$); Min-K\\% drops from 0.64 to 0.37 ($p = 0.003$).\nDIF's group-level conditioning is robust to the NLL-distribution changes induced by GRPO/PPO training.\n\n\\subsection{ConStat and DVD Comparisons}\n\\label{app:constat_dvd}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Detection method comparison. ConStat: 250 reformulated items, 50 models. DVD: 12 models $\\times$ 50 responses/item (69.6 GPU-hours). Semi-synthetic AUROC on injected ground truth.}\n\\label{tab:method_comparison}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{Flagged \\%} & \\textbf{Jaccard vs.\\ DIF} & \\textbf{AUROC} \\\\\n\\midrule\nDIF (MH, ETS C) & 31.3 & -- & 0.82 \\\\\nConStat (FDR) & 12.3 & 0.24 & 0.68 \\\\\nDVD & 13.2 & 0.16 & 0.61 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nEnsemble fusion of all three methods yields suboptimal results.\nMajority voting (2-of-3): TPR $= 0.35$, F1 $= 0.38$.\nWeighted fusion (DIF 0.58, ConStat 0.27, DVD 0.15): TPR $= 0.43$, F1 $= 0.43$.\nDIF alone achieves TPR $= 0.51$, higher than any ensemble configuration.\nThe coverage gain from fusion is only +4.2 percentage points, insufficient to justify the additional $\\sim$150 GPU-hours and open-weight model access required by DVD.\nThe three methods detect near-orthogonal item subsets, confirming that ensemble fusion is not beneficial when detection mechanisms are fundamentally heterogeneous.\n\n\\subsection{GSM8K Replication and GSM1K Correlation}\n\\label{app:gsm8k}\n\nGSM8K temporal DIF: C\\% $= 18.9$\\% (standard matching), 47.4\\% (external matching), random control 0.3\\%.\nThe standard-external Spearman correlation is 0.926, confirming high concordance between matching strategies.\nSemi-synthetic $\\Delta$TPR at $p = 1.0$: +0.818 (standard), +0.517 (external), exceeding MMLU due to GSM8K's more unidimensional structure.\n\n\\paragraph{GSM1K Correlation (Exploratory).}\nAmong 33 models with both GSM8K and GSM1K scores, DIF C\\% anti-correlates with the GSM8K-GSM1K gap: Pearson $r = -0.478$ ($p = 0.005$), partial $r = -0.385$ controlling for accuracy ($p = 0.027$), permutation $p = 0.014$.\nThe direction is \\emph{opposite} to the contamination hypothesis: models flagged more by DIF show \\emph{smaller} GSM8K-GSM1K gaps.\nThis is consistent with DIF capturing genuine capability improvement (which transfers to GSM1K) rather than benchmark-specific memorization.\nWe report this as exploratory given the small sample ($N = 33$) and wide confidence interval ($r \\in [-0.71, -0.16]$).\n\n\\subsection{Bifactor IRT Equating Pilot}\n\\label{app:bifactor}\n\nA pilot study assessed whether traditional psychometric test equating could address temporal instability.\nUnidimensional IRT: RMSEA $= 0.121$, CFI $= 0.776$.\nFive-group bifactor: RMSEA $= 0.094$, CFI $= 0.841$.\nFull 57-factor bifactor failed to converge (non-positive-definite Hessian after 500 EM iterations).\nUsing the 5-group model, 2,163 DIF-free anchor items (17.3\\%) were identified (ICC concordance $= 0.82$), but equated score correlation reached only $\\rho = 0.91$, below the target of 0.95.\nThis negative result confirms that MMLU's extreme multidimensionality violates essential unidimensionality, precluding traditional equating.\n\n\\subsection{Item Generation Calibration}\n\\label{app:itemgen}\n\nWe generated 400 replacement items across 10 subjects for DIF-flagged items.\nConstruct adherence (human evaluation): 78.4\\%.\nDifficulty calibration: prompt-based $\\rho = 0.38$, student-simulation $\\rho = 0.52$, both below the target of 0.75.\nHuman quality score: 3.2/5.0 (target: 4.0).\nThis negative result demonstrates that automated item regeneration is not a viable short-term remedy, reinforcing the diagnostic lens framing: DIF's value lies in audit and understanding, not automated item replacement.\n\n\\subsection{Bayesian Cumulative DIF}\n\\label{app:bayesian}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Cumulative Bayesian DIF power across 4 simulated administrations ($K = 500$ items, $N = 200$ models/admin, 100 true DIF items). FPR stable at 0.042--0.045 across all administrations.}\n\\label{tab:bayesian}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{ccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Administration} & \\textbf{TPR ($\\delta = 0.8$)} & \\textbf{TPR ($\\delta = 1.0$)} \\\\\n\\midrule\n1 & 0.22 & 0.41 \\\\\n2 & 0.38 & 0.58 \\\\\n3 & 0.53 & 0.72 \\\\\n4 & 0.65 & 0.81 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThis simulation supports the semi-annual monitoring recommendation: three cumulative assessments raise TPR from 0.41 to 0.72 for medium effects ($\\delta = 1.0$) while FPR remains $\\leq 0.045$.\nPosterior SD shrinks from 1.0 to 0.31 over four administrations, equivalent to a four-fold increase in measurement precision.\n\n\\subsection{Item-Focused Trees}\n\\label{app:ift}\n\nItem-Focused Trees (IFT) treat release date as a continuous covariate, avoiding MH's binary temporal split.\nIFT flags 28.7\\% of items (Jaccard $= 0.78$ with MH C class), confirming MH's categorical split captures the main temporal signal.\nChangepoint timing analysis: 71\\% of flagged items show a single changepoint (median: September 2023, coinciding with dense model releases including Llama-2, Mistral, and Phi-1), 12\\% show multiple changepoints, and 17\\% exhibit gradual drift.\n\n\\subsection{Odds Ratio Regression}\n\\label{app:regression}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Logistic regression decomposition of DIF status. Model 1: domain accuracy change only. Model 2: domain change + item properties. Simpson's paradox: OR for domain improvement reverses sign when conditioning on item properties.}\n\\label{tab:regression}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Predictor} & \\textbf{M1 OR} & \\textbf{M2 OR} & \\textbf{M1 $R^2$} & \\textbf{M2 $R^2$} \\\\\n\\midrule\nDomain $\\Delta$acc (per 0.10) & 1.55 & 0.73 & 0.0007 & \\multirow{3}{*}{0.042} \\\\\nDifficulty (per 0.10) & -- & 0.78 & -- & \\\\\nDiscrimination (per 0.10) & -- & 1.51 & -- & \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe sign reversal from Model 1 (OR $= 1.55$) to Model 2 (OR $= 0.73$) is a Simpson's paradox: DIF items tend to be harder and higher-discrimination, and these properties correlate with both DIF status and domain accuracy change.\nControlling for item properties reveals that domain improvement is, if anything, \\emph{negatively} associated with DIF.\nModel 1's $R^2 = 0.0007$ (McFadden) supports the ``$\\leq$7.6\\% explained'' claim.\nLikelihood ratio tests confirm that adding item properties significantly improves fit ($\\chi^2 = 647$, $p < 0.001$).\n\n\\subsection{Architecture Stratification}\n\\label{app:architecture}\n\nMETABENCH MMLU composition: 5,226 decoder-only (99.98\\%), 1 encoder-decoder (flan-ul2).\nDIF comparison: decoder-only C\\% $= 31.3$\\% $=$ full C\\%, Jaccard $= 1.0$, zero items changed classification.\nIRT fit: RMSEA $= 0.121$ for both full and decoder-only samples ($\\Delta = 0.0$).\nArchitecture heterogeneity is a non-issue for this dataset.\n\n\\end{document}\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false}}
